From: DKR-R Win95 port
Subject: [PATCH] Route synchronisation and the mod system through the platform seams

Windows 95 resolves every import at load time, and <mutex> pulls in six symbols
it does not export -- four of them condition variables introduced with Windows
Vista. Including the header is enough to stop the binary from starting, whether
or not anything is ever locked.

This adds librecomp/sync.hpp, alongside the fileio.hpp seam and mirroring
ultramodern/threading.hpp, and routes recomp.cpp, pi.cpp and mods.hpp through
it. Only mutex and lock_guard are required: librecomp guards shared tables and
never waits on a condition or starts a thread.

Note how this differs from the filesystem seam next to it. There the *type*
std::filesystem::path works on that platform and only its operations do not, so
the type stays and all 250 uses of it stay with it. Here the types themselves
are the problem, so the names change at every declaration. That is more
intrusive, and there is no way around it.

The mod system is included, which was not the original intent -- it is out of
scope as a feature -- but it is compiled and linked into the target, so its
forbidden includes would block the binary from loading just the same. A feature
that does not work is one thing; a binary that does not start is another. So
mod_manifest.cpp, mod_config_api.cpp and mods.cpp get the same treatment:
filesystem operations through recomp::fs, streams opened from narrow strings.

fileio.hpp grows the operations those files need -- absolute, is_directory,
is_regular_file, and list_directory in place of directory_iterator. absolute is
wrapped so that neither branch throws: two branches of one seam that differ on
error handling are worse than no seam at all.

